#EXTINF:337 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue I. Diogenes commissions Polydeukes- about to return to the upper world- to inform Menippus of the actual condition of things in the land of shades- and to deliver admonitory messages to various sorts of men-the rich- the powerful- the proud; and- finally- to the poor- whom- when they complain of their lot on Earth- he is to console by representing the complete equality which prevails in the regions of the dead. - Read by ToddHW - Duration 00:05:37
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_01_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:232 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue IV. Hermes demands from Charon arrears of payment due to him for his services on the styx. Charon excuses himself on the plea of bad times; no great war or famine- as it happened- ravaging the Earth at that moment. Hermes moralises on the causes of death- different from those of old- which despatch men in crowds to Hades. - Read by Algy Pug - Duration 00:03:52
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_04_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:270 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue V. Pluto directs Hermes to bring him the fortune and legacy-hunters and flatterers of a certain rich man- and to suffer the latter to outlive his fawning satellites. - Read by ToddHW - Duration 00:04:30
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_05_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:411 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue VI. Terpsion- a legacy-hunter- accuses Pluto and the Fates in that- although only thrity years of age- they had caused hime to predecease the object of his theder regards- the millionaire nonagenarian- Thukritus. Pluto convinces Terpsion of the injustice of his accusation; and the legacy-hunter consoles himself in the prospect of being soon joined in Hades by his late rivals on Earth. - Read by Alan Mapstone - Duration 00:06:51
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_06_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:203 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue VII. Zenophantes and Kallidemides- two parasites- bewail one to the other their fates- in having been in the midst of their scheming unexpectedly mismissed to Hades. Killidemides- in particular- recounts the pleasant manner in which he brought about his own death. - Read by Alan Mapstone - Duration 00:03:23
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_07_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:170 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue VIII. Knemon- a legacy-hunter- laments to his neighbour Damnippus- that- whereas he had publicly- in his will- bequeathed all his wealth to the millionaire Hermolaus- in the expectation that the latter would reciprocate the benefit- he- the speculating testator- by his sudden death- had been frustrated of all his hopes- and besides- had left his family destitute. - Read by Alan Mapstone - Duration 00:02:50
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_08_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:360 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue IX. Polystratus- a centenarian plutocrat- upon arriving in hades- narrates to his friend Simylus how- by reason of his great wealth- he had enjoyed the adulation of the world and an abundance of gifts from speculating flatterers- and how he had disappointed them all by his will. - Read by TriciaG - Duration 00:06:00
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_09_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:350 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue XI. Krates and Diogenes- meeting in Hades- indulge their satire on the subject of the fates of two millionaire merchants (cousins) who had been constantly plotting- in the usual manner- each for the other's legacy- and who had both perished on the same day by shipwreck. The two eminent cynics congratulate themselves on the recollection of the very different character of their own objects in life. - Read by ToddHW - Duration 00:05:50
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_11_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:399 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue XIII. Diogenes jeers at Alexander of Macedon for his late pretensions to divinity- at the same time satirizing the servile attitude of the conquered Greek states towards him. He proceeds to remind the arrogant Conqueror of all this vain power and glory- and casts large part of the blame on Alexander's preceptor Aristotle- for flattering and fostering the pride and ambition of his pupil. Diogenes- finally- recommends the dead Potentate to drink the waters of the river Lethe. - Read by ToddHW - Duration 00:06:39
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_13_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:409 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue XIV. Philip- King of Macedon- ridicules his son Alexander's absurd arrogance in claiming to the the son of Ammon- and calls in question the greatness of his military achievements. Alexander defends himself. - Read by Mike Manolakes - Duration 00:06:49
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_14_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:318 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue XV. Antilochus- the son of Nestor (one of the Greek heroes who fell during the siege of Ilium)- remonstrates whith his friend Achilleus for having given utterance to the words put into his mouth by the poet of the Odyssey-that he would rather be a slave on Earth than King in Hades-shows hime the uselessness of regrets in the under-world- and- at the same time- attempts to console him with the reflection that he is far from being alone in his fate. Achilleus takes the admonition of his friend in good part- but refuses to be comforted. - Read by Alan Mapstone - Duration 00:05:18
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_15_lucian_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:405 group-title="D",Dialogues Of The Dead — Lucian Of Samosata · Dialogue XVI. Diogenes- the cynic- expresses his astonishment to Herakles at seeing the son of Zeus in Hades- like the rest. That hero pretends that his actual self is in Heaven- while it is his eidolon- or phantom- which is among the dead. - Read by ToddHW - Duration 00:06:45
https://www.archive.org/download/lucians_dialogues_of_the_dead_2201_librivox/dialoguesofthedead_16_lucian_128kb.mp3
